From c8b243a0d8f214ed02250b3030bba744291185f2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Timo Hueser <timo.hueser@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 6 Aug 2026 23:09:08 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] feat: pre-allocate a file's cluster chain in FAT-block-sized
 runs
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

`VolumeManager::write` extends a file one cluster at a time, at the moment it
runs off the end, and each extension costs four single-block device writes:
`update_fat` twice (end-of-file on the new cluster, then the link from the old
tail), each mirrored across both FATs. On an SD card a single-block write is a
whole internal program cycle, so streaming a large file pays those cycles
*interleaved with the data* — exactly between the multi-block bursts the CMD25
batching exists to produce.

`VolumeManager::preallocate(file, len)` lets a caller that already knows the
final length hoist all of it out of the streaming path, and makes the allocation
itself much cheaper than the sum of the per-cluster ones: `FatVolume::
alloc_cluster_run` chains a run of consecutive free clusters inside **one** FAT
block and writes that block back once. One FAT32 block holds 128 entries, so a
4 MiB span of file costs the four writes that 32 KiB used to.

Runs stop at FAT-block boundaries and at the first non-free entry, which is what
keeps the one-block `BlockCache` sound: a run that stayed inside one block needs
no read between `read_mut` and `write_back`, so nothing can evict the dirty
block. The chain-tail link is written *before* the run's own block goes dirty,
for the same reason.

Pre-allocation deliberately does not change the file's length — only `write`
does — and returns short rather than failing when the volume fills, so an
optimistic caller can carry on and let the write path report `DiskFull`.

tests/preallocate.rs pins the claim on the RAM-disk image: writing 256 KiB costs
511 single-block writes in the write path without pre-allocation and 1 with it,
with byte-identical read-back. Short writes into a reservation, double
reservations and a second file allocated afterwards are covered too.
